    "result_text": "Tests green and the serialized JSON is unchanged (`event` still present).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Primary finding \u2014 agreed and fixed.** The `event` discriminator is now owned by the record. `OwnerCreatedEvent` gains a four-argument convenience constructor that delegates to the canonical one, stamping `OWNER_CREATED` itself; the `OWNER_CREATED` constant is now `private`. The caller in `AuditOwnerCreated` no longer passes (nor can pass) the marker, so the event can't be constructed with an inconsistent value. Jackson still serializes all five record components, so the emitted JSON is byte-for-byte the same \u2014 `{\"seq\":...,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`.\n\n**Secondary finding \u2014 agreed it's a latent coherence risk, not a blocker; addressed via documentation.** The reviewer is right that if only `primaryIdentifier` flips to a memberId, a field still named `customerCode` would hold a memberId. The field name is correct for today's spec, so I kept it, but I updated the record's javadoc to state explicitly that the eventual memberId change must rename this component alongside flipping `primaryIdentifier`, so the name and value stay coherent. No behaviour change.\n\nFull `AcceptanceTest` passes \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
