        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe split into a dedicated immutable `OwnerCreatedEvent` record plus a single `primaryIdentifier` resolution point is a good, idiomatic shape, and `AuditOwnerCreated` stays a thin two-method unit. One structural issue keeps it from being clean:\n\n**The event's own discriminator is threaded through the caller.** `OWNER_CREATED` is an intrinsic property of what an `OwnerCreatedEvent` *is*, yet the record accepts it as a constructor component and the caller passes `OwnerCreatedEvent.OWNER_CREATED` back into `new OwnerCreatedEvent(...)`. That round-trip is redundant and lets the type be constructed with an inconsistent `event` value. The record should own this itself \u2014 e.g. a convenience constructor that omits `event` and fills it, or a compact/canonical constructor that always sets it \u2014 so Jackson still serializes the `event` field but no caller can (or has to) supply it. That also removes the need for the public `OWNER_CREATED` constant to be part of the call site.\n\nSecondary, worth a second look but defensible under the current spec:\n\n- The record component and JSON key are named `customerCode`, but the value is sourced from `primaryIdentifier(owner)` and the javadoc promises that flipping that method alone makes the event \"carry the memberId.\" Structurally the value and the field name will then disagree (a `customerCode` key holding a memberId), which undercuts the \"change only one point\" claim for anyone reading the emitted JSON. The field name is correct for today's spec, so this is a latent coherence risk to flag rather than a blocker \u2014 just make sure the eventual memberId change is understood to touch the field name too, not only `primaryIdentifier`."
